    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Your bedroom is more than just a place to sleep; it is your retreat from the world, a personal sanctuary where your day begins and ends. Decorating this space means balancing high-end aesthetics with soothing, practical comfort. Whether you are starting from scratch in a new home or refreshing a tired space, this guide walks you through every step of the design process to create a room that reflects your personality and helps you properly unwind.</p>

<h2 id="aesthetic">1. Aesthetic</h2>
<p>Before you move a single piece of furniture, define the mood of your room. Are you drawn to the clean lines of Scandinavian minimalism, the warmth of rustic farmhouse style or the bold drama of art deco? Deciding on a cohesive aesthetic early stops the room feeling disjointed. Use Pinterest or a physical mood board to collect images that resonate with you, and look for patterns in what you save: do you prefer dark, moody walls or bright, airy spaces? Once you have a vision, it becomes a north star for every purchase you make.</p>

<h2 id="palette">2. Palette</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-panelled-headboard-cove-light|A soft neutral palette, backlit panelled headboard and simple bench keep this bedroom calm and restful.-->
<p>Colour is perhaps the most powerful tool in your decorating kit. In a bedroom the goal is usually calm. Many people find that cool colours such as soft blues, muted greens and lavenders feel restful and help them wind down. If you prefer warmth, stick to earthy neutrals like sand, terracotta or taupe. Overly stimulating colours such as bright red or neon yellow can make it harder to relax. Remember that neutral does not have to mean white: a deep navy or forest green can act as a sophisticated neutral that adds real depth to the space. Our <a href="/blog/bedroom-colour-guide">bedroom colour guide</a> explores palettes in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="bed-frame">3. Bed frame</h2>
<!--figure:curved-upholstered-bed|A curved bouclé bed against a textured plaster wall shows how the bed frame sets the style of the whole room.-->
<p>The bed is the undisputed star of the room, and its size and style dictate the flow of the entire space. In a large room, a four-poster bed or a grand upholstered headboard can fill the volume and create a sense of luxury. In smaller rooms, a low-profile platform bed or a sleek metal frame keeps the space feeling open. Beyond looks, consider height: a taller bed often feels more traditional and formal, while a lower bed leans towards modern and casual.</p>

<h2 id="linens">4. Linens</h2>
<p>We spend a large part of our lives in bed, so this is the place to invest in quality. Layering is the secret to a magazine-ready bed. Start with good cotton or breathable linen sheets, add a duvet or quilt for warmth, then layer a throw at the foot of the bed for texture. Do not forget the pillows: use a mix of sleeping pillows, Euro shams for height and one or two decorative lumbar cushions to tie the colour scheme together. Mixing textures, such as silk with wool, adds visual interest without needing loud patterns. For more ideas, see our <a href="/blog/bed-styling-guide">bed styling guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="lighting">5. Lighting</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-panelled-chandelier|A glass chandelier above a tufted bed acts as a statement piece in this moody panelled bedroom.-->
<p>Bedroom lighting should be layered. You need three types: ambient (the main overhead light), task (reading lamps or wall lights) and accent (mood lighting such as LED strips or candles). Avoid harsh, bright white bulbs and choose warm white or soft white instead to create a cosy atmosphere. Dimmer switches make a big difference in the bedroom, letting you move from getting-ready brightness to winding-down softness. If you have the space, a statement chandelier can serve as a stunning piece of jewellery for the room.</p>

<h2 id="rugs">6. Rugs</h2>
<p>A rug anchors the room and gives you a soft landing for your feet in the morning. The most common mistake is choosing a rug that is too small. Ideally, it should extend at least 45 to 60 cm (18 to 24 inches) beyond the sides and foot of the bed. If you have beautiful wooden floors you do not want to cover, consider layering a smaller, plush sheepskin or a vintage runner alongside the bed. Texture is key here: natural fibres like jute or wool add warmth and organic appeal.</p>

<h2 id="art">7. Art</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-ring-chandelier-tufted-headboard|Abstract gold artwork above a channel-tufted headboard gives this beige bedroom a clear focal point.-->
<p>Personalise your sanctuary with artwork that speaks to you. Above the bed is the classic spot for a large statement piece or a curated gallery wall. When choosing art, consider scale: a tiny frame on a large wall will look lost. For a gallery wall, keep the frames in one colour for a cohesive look, or mix and match for an eclectic feel. Beyond paintings, consider woven tapestries, mirrors to bounce light or even architectural wall moulding. Our <a href="/blog/headboard-and-bedroom-accent-wall-ideas">headboard and bedroom accent wall ideas</a> offer more inspiration.</p>

<h2 id="organisation">8. Organisation</h2>
<p>Clutter makes it hard to relax, so effective storage is essential for a peaceful bedroom. Choose bedside tables with drawers to hide charging cables, lotions and books. If your wardrobe is small, a beautiful chest of drawers or an armoire can provide extra space while acting as a design element. In smaller rooms, look for hidden storage, such as a bed frame with built-in drawers or decorative baskets that slide under furniture.</p>

<h2 id="greenery">9. Greenery</h2>
<p>Bringing a little of the outdoors in can instantly breathe life into a bedroom. Plants add a pop of natural colour, and many people find them uplifting to wake up to. If you have green fingers, a large ficus or a trailing pothos on a high shelf can create a lush, jungle-like feel. If you are worried about maintenance, snake plants and ZZ plants are very hardy and cope well with low light. Even a simple vase of fresh eucalyptus on the bedside table can bring a spa-like scent and look.</p>

<h2 id="curtains">10. Curtains</h2>
<!--figure:symmetrical-panelled-bedroom|Full-length drapes frame the windows in this symmetrical bedroom with a tall upholstered wall panel.-->
<p>Window treatments are the eyelashes of the room: they finish the look. Blackout curtains are well worth considering for better sleep, especially if you live in a city with streetlights. You can layer them with sheer panels to let soft, filtered light in during the day. Hang your curtain pole higher and wider than the window frame; this tricks the eye into reading the windows as larger and the ceiling as higher, giving the room a grander, airier feel.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> As a rough guide, fix the curtain pole about 10 to 15 cm (4 to 6 inches) above the window frame, or close to the ceiling, and let it extend 15 to 25 cm (6 to 10 inches) beyond each side so the curtains can stack back clear of the glass.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="seating">11. Seating</h2>
<!--figure:guest-ready-grey-bedroom|A tufted bench at the foot of the bed and a pair of armchairs turn this calm grey bedroom into a place to sit as well as sleep.-->
<p>If space allows, adding a seating area transforms your bedroom from a place to sleep into a multi-functional suite. A velvet armchair in the corner, a bench at the foot of the bed or even a small bistro set by the window gives you somewhere to read, put on shoes or enjoy a morning coffee. It makes the bedroom feel like a destination rather than just a functional room.</p>

<h2 id="hardware">12. Hardware</h2>
<p>Do not overlook the small details. Swapping the standard plastic or cheap metal handles on your chest of drawers and bedside tables for good-quality brass, matt black or leather pulls can make inexpensive furniture look custom-made. Match your hardware to your light fittings for a polished, designer look, or mix metals (like black and gold) for a more modern, curated feel.</p>

<h2 id="conclusion">Conclusion</h2>
<p>Decorating a bedroom is a journey towards a space that supports your wellbeing. By focusing on a cohesive palette, layering textures and prioritising comfort, you can turn any room into a high-end retreat. The best-decorated rooms are not just beautiful; they reflect the people who live in them. Take your time, choose pieces you love and enjoy the process of building your perfect sanctuary.</p>
